{
  "outcome": "escalated",
  "reply_to_user": "Sorry about the rough morning, James. We've passed both issues to our Tier 2 team because your desktop and the floor 2 printer are both showing offline, which may point to a wider problem on the floor rather than something on your machine alone. While you wait, could you check that the network cable is firmly plugged into your desktop and the link light is on? If someone is near the floor 2 printer, could they check its screen for any error message (paper jam, out of paper or toner, door open) and power it off for 30 seconds, then back on? If you can't get your desktop online, please let us know, and if you need to print today, use another printer on a different floor.",
  "internal_note": "Sender: James O'Connor (user_id 8), Operations, active, not blocked, VPN enabled, failed logins 0, usual hours 06:00-14:00 America/Chicago. Only device: BL-DT-0108 (Windows 11), check_device_status shows online:false, last check-in 2026-10-11 01:41 UTC, no warnings, disk 78% free. Printer BL-PR-FL2 shows online:false for everyone, last check-in 2026-10-10 22:41 UTC, no warnings. Both the desktop and the printer are offline on the same floor, so this may be a floor network or switch outage affecting several people; not confirmed. KB-022 (printer offline) applies: printer offline for everyone, so the printer is the problem, but its on-site power cycle and cable check are not something I can do remotely and it has not been done yet. Escalation criterion met: no network for the desktop is not covered by any KB article (KB-014 is Wi-Fi-connected-no-internet, not a wired/no-link case), and the printer may need a network or hardware check. Please check whether other floor 2 users are also offline and whether the floor switch or patch panel is affected. No fixes were queued and no account actions taken.",
